        "thinking": "`client.ts` uses `node:fs` (via `readFile`, `readdir`) and `fileURLToPath` — these are Node-only APIs that won't work in Workers. But the key thing is: `client.ts` does NOT import `embedded-postgres` directly.\n\nThe `embedded-postgres` issue comes from:\n1. `db/src/index.ts` re-exports `getEmbeddedPostgresTestSupport` from `test-embedded-postgres.ts`\n2. `db/src/index.ts` re-exports `createEmbeddedPostgresLogBuffer` from `embedded-postgres-error.ts` (which imports `embedded-postgres`)\n\n\n3. Both `embedded-postgres-error.ts` and `test-embedded-postgres.ts` directly import the `embedded-postgres` package, so even though these exports are test-only utilities, they're being bundled into the main entry point.\n\nThe real issue is that esbuild's tree-shaking works at the module level—if a file is imported, all its code executes at load time unless explicitly marked as side-effect-free. Since `db/src/index.ts` re-exports from these files, they get evaluated regardless of whether those functions are actually called. The solution is to add conditional exports in the package.json so the CF worker gets a build that excludes these test utilities entirely.\n\nBut that's a bigger refactor. The quicker approach is to create a custom esbuild script that marks `embedded-postgres` as external, then configure wrangler to use that build instead of its default bundling.\n\nI'm weighing the tradeoffs between a custom build script, upgrading wrangler, fixing the package exports with conditions, or restructuring the db package to separate node-specific code into a subpath—each has different complexity and maintenance costs.\n\nLooking at what's actually exported from the main index file, I can see it includes functions for creating databases, managing embedded postgres, running backups, and handling errors.\n\nThe real issue is that `client.ts` might be pulling in Node-only dependencies too, but the embedded postgres problem specifically comes from `test-embedded-postgres.ts` and `embedded-postgres-error.ts`. Let me trace through the imports to see what's actually causing the browser incompatibility, starting with checking whether `embedded-postgres-error.ts` even depends on the embedded postgres library itself.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
    ],
